Here $h$ is Planck’s constant. Emission in discrete portions alone is not a complete explanation of light–matter interactions.
Discrete falling raindrops do not imply that a river consists only of indivisible drops. The photoelectric effect supplies evidence for absorption of energy in quanta of $E=h\nu$, as in equation (10.1);
this does not make photons classical little balls.

The experiment on page 225 of the source uses zinc and compares its work function with aluminum and iron. Such comparisons depend on the actual surfaces;
the source’s simple ordering is not a universal rule for all samples.
A surface with a smaller work function has a longer limiting wavelength $\lambda_m$. The source gives $\lambda_m=6.8\times10^{-5}$ cm as an approximate value for its sodium sample.
